TASK 2 — Student KAFKA-TO- Student BDI MIGRATION
======================================================================

2A. NEXT RUN START TIME CALCULATION ALGORITHM

Purpose: determine the event-fetch starting timestamp for the next processing run.

RULES
  1. Check whether any run is IN_PROGRESS. If yes, do not start a concurrent run.
  2. Otherwise find the MAX(run_id) (the latest run as defined in this design).
  3. Within that run, find MAX(iteration_id) WHERE iteration_status = 'SUCCESS'.
     IMPORTANT: select the latest SUCCESSFUL iteration, not merely the latest iteration regardless of status.
  4. If a successful iteration exists, next_run_start_time = its successful iteration_end_time.
  5. If no successful iteration exists in the latest run, next_run_start_time = latest_run.run_start_time.
  6. If no run exists yet, next_run_start_time = configured initial start time.
  7. Use this boundary for the next event fetch.

PSEUDOCODE
  if exists_run_with_status('IN_PROGRESS'):
      STOP
  latest_run = get_run_with_max_run_id()
  if latest_run is None:
      next_run_start_time = configured_initial_start_time
  else:
      success_iteration = get_iteration_with_max_id(
          run_id=latest_run.run_id, status='SUCCESS')
      if success_iteration is not None:
          next_run_start_time = success_iteration.iteration_end_time
      else:
          next_run_start_time = latest_run.run_start_time

FLOW
Start -> Any run IN_PROGRESS? -> YES: Stop
                            -> NO: MAX run_id found?
                                   -> NO: Initial configured time
                                   -> YES: MAX successful iteration_id found?
                                          -> YES: Its success end time
                                          -> NO: Latest run start time
                          -> Start next run from selected time.

SAFETY NOTE
The selected timestamp must be a safe replay boundary. Verify event timestamp semantics, inclusivity (> or >=), pagination, deduplication/idempotency, and late-arriving events so that failures do not cause dropped events. MAX(run_id) assumes IDs correctly represent run order.

